{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"fceafaca7fd64840","name":"card_event_betasom","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Italy/events/card_event_betasom","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"BETASOM","text":"Develop a Navy card. The top card of your deck has the Navy type until the end of your next turn.","flavorText":"BETASOM, an acronym for \"Bordeaux Sommergibile\", was an Italian submarine base established in Bordeaux, France, during WWII. Operational from August 1940 to September 1943, it served as the headquarters for Italian naval operations in the Atlantic. The base housed up to thirty submarines and included facilities such as dry docks and interconnected basins. Approximately 1,600 personnel were stationed there, including a guard unit from the San Marco Regiment. Under the command of Admiral Angelo Parona and later successors, Italian submarines launched patrols targeting Allied shipping lanes. Despite initial challenges, including design limitations of their submarines, Italian forces adapted and achieved notable successes. By August 1943, they had sunk 109 Allied ships, totaling over 590,000 tons. However, coordination with German U-boat operations was limited, and the Italians often operated independently. Following Italy's armistice in September 1943, the base ceased operations. Some Italian submarines were scuttled, while others were taken over by German forces. Today, the former BETASOM base serves as a cultural venue, preserving its historical significance.","faction":"Italy","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.develop"],"Name":"card_event_betasom","title":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_betasom_title","SourceString":"BETASOM","LocalizedString":"BETASOM"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Italy","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_betasom_text","SourceString":"Develop a Navy card. The top card of your deck has the Navy type until the end of your next turn.","LocalizedString":"Develop a Navy card. The top card of your deck has the Navy type until the end of your next turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_betasom_history","SourceString":"BETASOM, an acronym for \"Bordeaux Sommergibile\", was an Italian submarine base established in Bordeaux, France, during WWII. Operational from August 1940 to September 1943, it served as the headquarters for Italian naval operations in the Atlantic. The base housed up to thirty submarines and included facilities such as dry docks and interconnected basins. Approximately 1,600 personnel were stationed there, including a guard unit from the San Marco Regiment. Under the command of Admiral Angelo Parona and later successors, Italian submarines launched patrols targeting Allied shipping lanes. Despite initial challenges, including design limitations of their submarines, Italian forces adapted and achieved notable successes. By August 1943, they had sunk 109 Allied ships, totaling over 590,000 tons. However, coordination with German U-boat operations was limited, and the Italians often operated independently. Following Italy's armistice in September 1943, the base ceased operations. Some Italian submarines were scuttled, while others were taken over by German forces. Today, the former BETASOM base serves as a cultural venue, preserving its historical significance.","LocalizedString":"BETASOM, an acronym for \"Bordeaux Sommergibile\", was an Italian submarine base established in Bordeaux, France, during WWII. Operational from August 1940 to September 1943, it served as the headquarters for Italian naval operations in the Atlantic. The base housed up to thirty submarines and included facilities such as dry docks and interconnected basins. Approximately 1,600 personnel were stationed there, including a guard unit from the San Marco Regiment. Under the command of Admiral Angelo Parona and later successors, Italian submarines launched patrols targeting Allied shipping lanes. Despite initial challenges, including design limitations of their submarines, Italian forces adapted and achieved notable successes. By August 1943, they had sunk 109 Allied ships, totaling over 590,000 tons. However, coordination with German U-boat operations was limited, and the Italians often operated independently. Following Italy's armistice in September 1943, the base ceased operations. Some Italian submarines were scuttled, while others were taken over by German forces. Today, the former BETASOM base serves as a cultural venue, preserving its historical significance."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnAfterDeckChanged","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Develop"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_2'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_2.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Italy/NavalWarfare/t_betasom.t_betasom","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Look and Learn","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/BETASOM.BETASOM","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":408.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_betasom_photo","SourceString":"Italian submarine Archimede entering Betasom base in Bordeaux, France. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"Italian submarine Archimede entering Betasom base in Bordeaux, France. Date unknown."}},"variables":[{"name":"affectedCardID","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_betasom","owner":"card_event_betasom_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | 